TALENT_LAW.push({t:"Section 158 (general positive action): where an organisation reasonably thinks people sharing a protected characteristic suffer disadvantage, have different needs, or have disproportionately low participation, it may take proportionate action to address that. Applies in England, Scotland and Wales.",q:"This Act does not prohibit P from taking any action which is a proportionate means of achieving the aim of—",src:[SRCU("Equality Act 2010, section 158","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/158","")]},{t:"Section 159 (recruitment and promotion tie-break): favouring a candidate with the protected characteristic is allowed only where they are as qualified as the other candidate, there is no blanket policy of preference, and the action is proportionate.",q:"A is as qualified as B to be recruited or promoted,",src:[SRCU("Equality Act 2010, section 159","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/159","")]},{t:"Section 158 does not cover the tie-break, which is governed only by section 159.",q:"does not apply to",src:[SRCU("Equality Act 2010, section 158(4)","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2010/15/section/158","")]},{t:"Limits: positive discrimination, including quotas and recruiting solely because of a protected characteristic, is unlawful. Employers need evidence of disadvantage or low participation, should review schemes regularly, and should take legal advice.",q:"Positive discrimination is unlawful in Great Britain.",src:[SRCU("Positive action in the workplace (GOV.UK, updated 22 January 2026)","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/positive-action-in-the-workplace-guidance-for-employers/positive-action-in-the-workplace","")]},{t:"EHRC employer guidance: positive action must be shown to be appropriate and carefully thought through. Page last updated 27 March 2019.",q:"You must be able to show that positive action is an appropriate way for your organisation to achieve one of these aims and the steps you are taking have been carefully thought through.",src:[SRCU("Employers: what is positive action in the workplace? (EHRC)","https://www.equalityhumanrights.com/guidance/business/employers-what-positive-action-workplace","")]},{t:"Northern Ireland is outside the Equality Act 2010's employment provisions. Positive action there rests on the Race Relations (NI) Order 1997, the Fair Employment and Treatment (NI) Order 1998 and related law. Employers may encourage applications but may not reserve jobs.",q:"are only permitted to encourage members of the targeted group, or groups, to apply to work for you",src:[SRCU("Outreach Positive Action: A Guide to the Law and Good Practice for Employers (ECNI, undated archive copy)","https://libdeposit.qub.ac.uk/bitstream/NIOPA/3236/1/PositiveActionEmployerGuide.pdf","")]});
